Point 1 — keyed matching: When child elements or components carry a 'key' field, the reconciler matches old and new children by identity via those keys rather than by position. If the same key appears at a different position after a re-render, the existing node is moved rather than rebuilt — the stable identity token stays the same for the moved node, and references bound to keyed components continue resolving to the same instance even after reordering. On your unkeyed/positional sub-question: the documented contract is that keys give identity 'rather than by position'; no additional unkeyed edge-case behavior is documented, and you will not invent any. Note also the interaction with C005: a child component without update:true gets replaced with a fresh token regardless of position.

Point 2 — token persistence: Each real DOM element node is assigned its token the first time it is serialized, format #n<integer> from a monotonically incrementing counter. Once assigned, the same token is reused for that node at all future observation points. So yes — a moved keyed node keeps its old token; the reorder is expressed purely by the order in which children appear in the serialized output.

