You submitted this twice; I treat it as one question set, and it still overshoots the three-point budget. First three points only.

Point 1 — set visibility. The documented mechanism is the update step, not the mutation. Feature2_1's own case description states it: 'then change that state and apply a scheduled update. The update re-renders the component and diffs the new tree against the previous one, patching only what changed.' Accordingly, every single public mutation case follows the pattern set -> updateAsync/updateSync -> probe. Not one public case probes directly after a bare set. So per the documented contract: an update step must intervene for the change to appear at an observation point; a bare set has no documented probe-visible effect.

Point 2 — verbatim public case feature2_1_text_content_update (full artifact, unedited):
{
  "description": "Render a component whose text content is derived from a piece of mutable state, then change that state and apply a scheduled update. The update re-renders the component and diffs the new tree against the previous one, patching only what changed. Two observation points are reported: the serialized DOM before the change and after the update is flushed. The stable identity tokens reveal that the existing element is patched in place rather than rebuilt.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "kind": "component",
        "root": {
          "state": {
            "greeting": "Hello"
          },
          "render": {
            "el": "div",
            "children": [
              {
                "var": "greeting"
              },
              " World"
            ]
          }
        },
        "steps": [
          {
            "probe": "before"
          },
          {
            "set": {
              "greeting": "Goodbye"
            }
          },
          {
            "updateAsync": true
          },
          {
            "probe": "after"
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "== before ==\nelement=<div#n1>Hello World</div>\ntext=Hello World\n== after ==\nelement=<div#n1>Goodbye World</div>\ntext=Goodbye World\n"
    }
  ]
}
Note the after block: same token #n1 — patched in place, not rebuilt.

Point 3 — update:true versus omitted. A component definition with 'update: true' signals it can be patched in place when its parent re-renders it with new inputs. If the same definition (by name) appears at the same position and update is true, the engine updates the existing instance and reuses its DOM element — same identity token. If update is false or omitted, the engine cannot patch in place: it discards the old instance and builds a fresh one, producing a new identity token. Evidence: feature3_2 (update:true, token #n2 persists) versus feature3_3 (no update flag, child token flips #n2 -> #n4). Your sub-question — whether an update-capable child re-renders when its props are UNCHANGED — I don't have specific details on that aspect documented right now — could you check the existing test case files in rcb_tests/public_test_cases/ or the PRD in start.md? Those should have the authoritative spec. If something is still unclear after that, flag it and we can discuss as a team.

