Read the three blocks carefully: p1->p2 same definition A, ref name moves child->kid, SAME token #n2 (instance kept, binding moved). p3 switches definition A->B, fresh token #n3 (new instance), nested 'self' ref re-binds to the new element. That is the complete documented evidence for your token-counter question. Now go build.

Three artifacts, three points — you are exactly at budget. Dumping verbatim. Scheduler semantics for context: async mode batches submitted requests and executes them all at the animation-frame flush in submission order; requests submitted during the flush are drained within the SAME flush, never deferred. Sync mode runs the request immediately and drains everything it enqueues (async and sync alike) within the same flush in submission order.

Artifact 1 — feature4_1_async_batch:
{
  "description": "Submit several update requests to the deferred update scheduler. None of them run immediately; they are batched and all executed, in submission order, when the next animation frame is flushed. Each request emits a distinct label when it runs; the output is the sequence of emitted labels in execution order, one per line.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "kind": "scheduler",
        "mode": "async",
        "requests": [
          {
            "emit": 1
          },
          {
            "emit": 2
          },
          {
            "emit": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "1\n2\n3\n"
    }
  ]
}

Artifact 2 — feature4_2_async_nested:
{
  "description": "Submit a single deferred update request that, while running, submits further deferred update requests, which in turn submit more. All requests submitted during the same flush are drained within that same animation frame rather than being deferred to a later one. Each request emits a distinct label; the output is the labels in execution order, one per line.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "kind": "scheduler",
        "mode": "async",
        "requests": [
          {
            "emit": 1,
            "thenAsync": [
              {
                "emit": 2,
                "thenAsync": [
                  {
                    "emit": 3
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "1\n2\n3\n"
    }
  ]
}
Note: nested thenAsync requests during a flush produce the same output as a flat batch — drain-in-same-flush is the entire point.

Artifact 3 — feature4_3_sync_immediate:
{
  "description": "Submit an update request to the scheduler in synchronous mode, which runs it immediately. While running, that request submits both a deferred request and another synchronous request. Because a flush is already in progress, the additionally submitted requests are drained within the same immediate flush in submission order rather than waiting for an animation frame. Each request emits a distinct label; the output is the labels in execution order, one per line.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "kind": "scheduler",
        "mode": "sync",
        "requests": [
          {
            "emit": 1,
            "thenAsync": [
              {
                "emit": 2
              }
            ],
            "thenSync": [
              {
                "emit": 3
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected_output": "1\n2\n3\n"
    }
  ]
}
Note for your implementation: in sync mode a thenAsync follow-up does NOT wait for an animation frame — the in-progress flush drains it. Structure schema is 'emit' plus optional 'thenAsync' and 'thenSync' arrays of the same request shape, recursively. That is the whole feature4 surface. Go build.

